/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186511265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511395 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9ae32f88-30e5-5210-86d4-1850ad11001c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511399 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511399 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511399 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511399 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511399 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511208 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511399 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511399 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511399 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186510855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511399 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a2a97630-0fc1-580b-b276-59f04f851b20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511401 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511401 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511401 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511401 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511401 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511401 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511401 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511401 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186510854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511401 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a60f7d70-b0fc-5169-a4bc-8635f6350892" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511404 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511404 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511404 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511404 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511404 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511404 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511404 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511404 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186510844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511404 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a7580df7-3ea9-5eba-8784-550ab709f1c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511466 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511466 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105280" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511466 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511466 #edb/ref 1071 "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}) & -2y{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511466 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1640e015-4c92-5aff-b3e3-71cc75c19c43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511466 #edb/ref 1074 "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}) & -2y{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-2y{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2})=0 \\\\ -2y{e}^{x}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n-2y{e}^{x}=0\\,⟹\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{0}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x & =0 \\\\ x(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,0)$ and $(-2,0)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}))={e}^{x}({x}^{2}+4x-{y}^{2}+2)\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y{e}^{x})=-2y{e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}))=-2y{e}^{x}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y{e}^{x})=-2{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}+4x-{y}^{2}+2) & -2y{e}^{x} \\\\ -2y{e}^{x} & -2{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))=2\\cdot (-2)-{0}^{2}=-4<0,\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,0)$ is a saddle point.\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(-2,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2,0)=[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{-2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2{e}^{-2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(-2,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(-2,0)=det({f}^{″}(-2,0))=(-2{e}^{-2})^{2}-{0}^{2}=4{e}^{-4}>0\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(-2,0)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-2,0)=({f}^{″}(-2,0))_{11}=-2{e}^{-2}<0,$ the function $f$ has a local maximum at $(-2,0).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local maximum at $(-2,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511482 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511482 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103946" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511482 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511482 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the critical points of $f(x,y)=x{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511482 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3e112cc1-03f8-5d0c-87ca-e5043bf5d793" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511482 #edb/ref 1074 "The critical points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(a)$ does not exist, where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =(1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-2xy{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}(1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} & -2xy{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ such that ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ does not exist. Note that, in our case, ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so we just need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\n\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}=0 \\\\ -2xy{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\ne 0$ for any point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}1-2{x}^{2}=0 \\\\ -2xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n1-2{x}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these values into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})y=0\\,⟹\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0)$ and $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has two critical points, $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0)$ and $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0).$ Note that since ${f}^{′}(x)={0}^{T}$ at these two points, they are also both stationary points." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511496 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511496 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36799" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511496 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511496 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the stationary point of $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+4xy+{y}^{2}+4y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511496 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "524ccb21-d45d-53ab-aaf4-d88936372977" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511496 #edb/ref 1074 "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn tihs case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =4x+4y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =4x+2y+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4x+4y & 4x+2y+4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+4y=0 \\\\ 4x+2y+4=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n4x+4y=0\\,⟹\\,x=-y.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting $x=-y$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(-y)+2y+4 & =0 \\\\ -4y+2y+4 & =0 \\\\ -2y & =-4 \\\\ y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting this value into $x=-y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny=2\\,⟹\\,x=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has one solution, $(-2,2).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(-2,2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511526 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511401 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511526 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103926" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511526 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511526 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the critical points of $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}-4x+8y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511526 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8d70bf0e-d43a-5715-a8f9-251ae990c8fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511526 #edb/ref 1074 "The critical points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(a)$ does not exist, where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & ={y}^{2}-4 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2xy+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4 & 2xy+8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ such that ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ does not exist. Note that, in our case, ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so we just need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\n\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4=0 \\\\ 2xy+8=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$ in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n2xy+8=0\\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{4}{y}.\n$$\n\nNow, solving for $y$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-4=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into $x=-\\frac{4}{y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=-2 & \\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{4}{(-2)}=2 \\\\ y=2 & \\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{4}{2}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(2,-2)$ and $(-2,2).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has two critical points, $(2,-2)$ and $(-2,2).$ Note that since ${f}^{′}(x)={0}^{T}$ at these two points, they are also both stationary points." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511549 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511549 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36677" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511549 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511549 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the stationary point of $f(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511549 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c0460f02-5116-53e0-8333-fa6ec98b4c5e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511549 #edb/ref 1074 "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & 2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0 \\\\ 2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\ne 0$ for any point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has one solution, $(0,0).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(0,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
